  "query_preview": "..\nA dry weak trade-wind pattern is expected to continue through the\nmiddle of next week. During the afternoons, over the next few days, \nisland-effect showers may pop up along the western side of the \nislands. Overall, fairly benign weather is in store for the \nMarianas.\n\nMarine...\nLight to gentle east winds are expected for the rest of the week.\nSea state consists of a persistent trade swell. Combined seas of 3 \nto 4 will continue through the weekend. The trades may build around \nthe beginning of next week. Overall, fairly benign seas are expected\nthrough the middle of next week. \n\nEastern Micronesia...\nReally not much has changed from yesterday's thinking, as the models\ncontinue to be too aggressive in rainfall potential compared to\nobservations/analysis. In general, look for periods of scattered\nshowers to continue at Pohnpei and Kosrae, with isolated showers at\nMajuro, through the weekend. Then, some increase in surface\nconvergence and maybe ITCZ formation could lead to increasing\nrainfall potential early next week. For the mariners, light to gentle\nwinds and seas of 3 to 5 feet will continue through early next week.\n\nWestern Micronesia...\nSatellite imagery shows isolated showers across Yap and Palau, and\nscattered showers across Weno. Altimetry shows combined seas between\n3 and 5 feet. \n\nThe summer doldrums continue across western Micronesia. Shower\nactivity has decreased across Chuuk State and Weno this afternoon,\nbut low-end (30%) scattered showers are still seen on satellite.\nChuuk remains in a region of enhanced convergence from several\nfeatures including a weak surface low across western Yap State, a\nsurface high to the south, and a buffer circulation near the equator\nsouth of Pohnpei. Both the GFS and ECMWF show an uptick in showers\nand thunderstorms tonight, but they differ severity. The GFS is\nfavoring numerous showers, while the ECMWF is favoring low-end\nscattered showers. The current forecast is a slight lean towards the\nEuro with PoPs of 40% tonight. Both the GFS and ECMWF agree with\ncontinued low-level convergence over the next few days, which will\nsupport scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms through the\nweekend. \n\nFor Yap and Palau, the main feature is the weak surface low in\nwestern Yap State. For Yap, isolated showers are expected until the\nlow approaches this weekend, with scattered showers possible Saturday\nand Sunday. Meanwhile, low-level convergence is already becoming\nenhanced across Palau. Shower coverage looks to go from isolated to\nscattered overnight tonight. Light winds out of the north should help\nproduce island enhanced showers and thunderstorms on Friday, and\npossibly again on Saturday. \n\nNo changes were made to the marine forecast. Benign combined seas of\n3 to 5 feet look to prevail through the period.",
